{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggzZNWL3k95D5a2vzUWG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For [Co(ox)₃]³⁻, **Dq ≈ 1856 cm⁻¹**, **B ≈ 432 cm⁻¹** and **C ≈ 1946 cm⁻¹**. These come from the standard strong-field formulas for the two spin-allowed bands, worked out below.\n\n**Model**\n\nLow-spin octahedral d⁶ (t₂g⁶) has a ¹A₁g ground state. Both observed bands come from the t₂g⁵e_g¹ configuration. To first order in the strong-field limit:\n\n- ¹A₁g → ¹T₁g: E₁ = 10Dq − C\n- ¹A₁g → ¹T₂g: E₂ = 10Dq + 16B − C\n\n**Racah B**\n\nThe difference between the bands depends only on B:\n\nE₂ − E₁ = 16B = 23 529 − 16 611 = 6 918 cm⁻¹\n\nB = 6 918 / 16 ≈ **432 cm⁻¹**\n\n**C, using C/B = 4.5**\n\nC = 4.5 × 432.4 ≈ 1 946 cm⁻¹\n\n**Dq**\n\n10Dq = E₁ + C = 16 611 + 1 946 ≈ 18 557 cm⁻¹\n\nDq ≈ **1 856 cm⁻¹**\n\n**Checks**\n\n- β = B / B_free ≈ 432 / 1 100 ≈ 0.39, using about 1 100 cm⁻¹ for free Co³⁺. This shows a lot of nephelauxetic reduction.\n- Dq/B ≈ 4.3, which places the complex well on the low-spin side of the d⁶ crossover, as the model assumes.\n\nThese are the usual textbook first-order expressions. Diagonalising the full Tanabe–Sugano matrices, which adds mixing with the t₂g⁴e_g² ¹T states, would shift the values somewhat. I did not do that calculation.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1856, \"B\": 432, \"C\": 1946, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Used the strong-field low-spin d6 formulas: E(1T1g)=10Dq-C and E(1T2g)=10Dq+16B-C, with C=4.5B.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1673,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1673,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7ed49e0-adf1-4f25-b85e-8ce01c7a9